{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 demands a causal-mechanism story but never specifies the mechanism by which 'theory-quality × execution-fidelity' produces the comparative reliability verdict for the specific setup in the problem (panel of non-experts vs. single expert, verifiable outcomes). The claim that 'when the frozen theory is causally complete, the panel's adherence is the mechanism of reliability' asserts a label, not a mechanism: it does not identify the causal chain from non-expert panel execution of checklist items to more-reliable verifiable judgments, nor does it address why panel aggregation (rather than a single non-expert with the same checklist) is part of the mechanism. Without that, the proposed reliability gain could be an artifact of variance reduction rather than theory-quality, which the target never rules out.","target":"ca0ea2ef706171086cbf2a2d8a3229a9cc4727fb2fed1183427c180a08f8a512"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's distinguishing test presupposes that the causal structure producing verifiable outcomes is known well enough to construct adversarial items where 'surface features targeted by the checklist diverge from the causal structure.' But the problem asks for evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes genuine gain from compliance tracking; if the causal structure were already available to construct such adversarial items, the reliability question would be largely settled. The target provides no mechanism for obtaining or validating the adversarial set independently of the very checklist being tested, so the proposed test risks circularity and does not show how a single evaluation could yield the distinguishing evidence under the problem's epistemic constraints.","target":"ee6ac8b6431525274cc48b9d2135624acdfe49912d2b34376bd1224a1812ae72"}]}